Aim
To demonstrate dictionary and related functions in python 

Algorithm
Start the program.
Create a dictionary emp with keys: name, age, salary, department.
Print the dictionary.
Access and display individual elements using keys.
Add a new key-value pair (phone number).
Update the salary value.
Delete the department key.
Display keys, values, and items of the dictionary.
Use get() method to retrieve values.
Update multiple values using update() method.
Take input string from the user. 12. Initialize an empty dictionary frequency. 13. Traverse each character in the string:
If character exists in dictionary, increment count.
Else, add it with count 1.
Print each character with its frequency.
Stop.

CODE
# Dictionary creation
emp = {"name": "rahul", "age": 35, "sal": 50000, "dept": "IT"}

print("The dictionary is:", emp)

# Accessing elements
print("name:", emp["name"])
print("age:", emp["age"])
print("salary:", emp["sal"])
print("department:", emp["dept"])

# Adding element
emp["ph no"] = 9988776655
print("after adding:", emp)

# Updating element
emp["sal"] = 60000
print("after updating:", emp)

# Deleting element
del emp["dept"]
print("after deleting:", emp)

# Keys, values, items
print("keys:")
print(emp.keys())

print("values:")
print(emp.values())

print("items:")
print(emp.items())

# Using get()
print("name:", emp.get("name"))
print("dept:", emp.get("dept", "not available"))

# Update multiple values
emp.update({"age": 36, "ph no": 8567894043})
print("after update")
print(emp)

# Character frequency
text = input("enter the string:")
frequency = {}

for ch in text:
    if ch in frequency:
        frequency[ch] = frequency[ch] + 1
    else:
        frequency[ch] = 1

print("character frequency:")
for ch, count in frequency.items():
    print(ch, ":", count)

